WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.220
Node.js security review is not only about individual functions.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:13.240
Many risks appear in the connections between route definitions, middleware, dependencies, configuration, and runtime behavior.

3
00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:27.180
A practical AI-assisted review should inspect route registration, middleware order, authentication checks, authorization checks, input validation, output shaping, error handling, logging, and configuration.

4
00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:35.680
Ask the assistant to follow the request flow from route registration through middleware into the handler and then into the database or external service.

5
00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:42.840
Middleware order is security-relevant because the same route can be safe or unsafe depending on what runs before it.

6
00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:52.860
A body parser, authentication guard, rate limiter, CORS policy, tenant resolver, or error handler may protect some routes and not others.

7
00:00:52.960 --> 00:01:01.040
AI can help compare route groups, identify inconsistent guard usage, and find paths that look similar but behave differently.

8
00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:06.420
The reviewer should confirm those findings against the actual framework behavior and test coverage.

9
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:17.740
Node.js review should also include package.json, package-lock.json, install scripts, dependency additions, environment variables, and runtime flags.

10
00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:31.840
A pull request that changes CORS, debug mode, cookie settings, trusted proxy configuration, request size limits, or production error handling can alter the security boundary without touching a vulnerable-looking function.

11
00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:43.620
AI can summarize these changes and generate review questions, but scanner results, package metadata, and configuration assumptions should be verified through trusted tooling and source evidence.
